@database "task_switch"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: task_switch.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Task Switch"
@{h1}Task Switch

An agent that works in phases -- plan, then do, then check -- needs a
different instruction in each phase. Task Switch lets the agent say which
phase it is in: it calls @{tt}set_task@{utt}, and the plugin stores the
name in a session variable (@{tt}current_task@{utt} by default). The
agent's prompt template reads that variable, so from the next LLM call on
the agent sees the instructions for the new phase. A switch can be guarded:
before it is allowed, another tool is asked whether the work of the
current phase is done. A second tool, @{tt}set_context@{utt}, sets any
other session variable the same way. The variables are kept with the
session and come back when it is reopened. It has no panel and no hook.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Switching the task  " link set_task}
  @{"  Gate checks  " link gates}
  @{"  Setting context variables  " link set_context}
  @{"  Where the state lives  " link state}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is not in the default configuration. An agent that uses it
brings its own instance in its YAML; this one comes with the debate
moderator:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    debate_switch:
      type: task_switch
      enabled: true
      config:
        task_var_name: "debate_phase"
        allowed_tasks:
          - setup
          - discussion
          - consensus
          - concluded
@{body}

The agent sees the tools when its allowlist names them, and its prompt
template decides what each task means:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  template_vars:
    debate_phase: "setup"
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+debate_switch/*"
  system_prompt: |
    {% if debate_phase == 'setup' %}Pick the participants.
    {% elif debate_phase == 'discussion' %}Run the rounds.
    {% endif %}
@{body}

The value in @{tt}template_vars@{utt} is where a new session starts. The
tools are named after the instance: @{tt}debate_switch_set_task@{utt},
@{tt}debate_switch_set_context@{utt}. No extra packages.

@{h2}Server settings

The settings go under @{tt}config:@{utt} in the server entry; keys directly
in the entry are not read. They are read once, when the plugin starts.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
task_var_name | current_task | the session variable set_task writes
allowed_tasks | none: any name | the task names set_task accepts; they are also sent to the model as the only choices
task_preconditions | none | a gate check per task name; see @{" Gate checks " link gates}
@{body}

A value of the wrong kind (@{tt}allowed_tasks@{utt} that is not a list,
@{tt}task_preconditions@{utt} that is not a mapping) is ignored without a
word. The plugin stores no files of its own; the variables go into the
session record (see @{" Where the state lives " link state}).
@endnode

@node set_task "Switching the task"
@{h1}Switching the task

@{tt}<instance>_set_task@{utt} sets the task variable of the calling
session.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
task_name | the task to switch to; required. Leading and trailing blanks are dropped, a number is taken as text
@{body}

What happens, in order:

@{bullet}An empty name is refused.
@{bullet}With @{tt}allowed_tasks@{utt} set, a name not in the list is
refused. The comparison is exact: @{tt}Review@{utt} is not
@{tt}review@{utt}.
@{bullet}A task with a @{" gate check " link gates} runs it; a closed
gate blocks the switch and changes nothing.
@{bullet}The variable is set for the session and written to the session
record. Switching to the task the session is already in is allowed and
does the same.

@{h2}Answers

@{table}
status | Fields
success | previous_task (the session's value before, @{tt}init@{utt} when it had none), current_task, persisted (the session record was written)
blocked | task_name, gate_closed (true), reason, gate_field, gate_value, precondition_result (the gate tool's full answer); the last three are null when the gate could not be checked
error | error: "task_name is required", or "Invalid task 'x'. Allowed tasks: ['plan', 'do']"
@{body}

The chat's status line shows @{i}Task: plan → do@{ui}; a blocked switch
shows @{i}Gate closed for task 'do': <reason>@{ui} as an error.

@{code json}
{"task_name": "review"}
{"status": "success", "previous_task": "execute", "current_task": "review", "persisted": true}
@{body}

@{tt}persisted: false@{utt} means the value holds for this run but is not
in the session record yet: there is no session store, or the write failed
(logged as a warning). The record catches up when the session is saved.
@endnode

@node gates "Gate checks"
@{h1}Gate checks

A gate keeps an agent from moving on before the work of its phase is
done. It is configured per task name:

@{code yaml}
config:
  task_var_name: "phase"
  task_preconditions:
    execute:
      tool: "project_status_check"
      params:
        project_id: "{{ project_id }}"
        scope: "plan"
      gate_field: "summary.can_proceed"
      error_field: "summary.gate_reason"
@{body}

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
tool | -- | the full tool name (server prefix included) to ask
params | none | its arguments; see below
gate_field | can_proceed | the field of the answer that must be true; dots go into nested objects
error_field | gate_reason | the field that says why not
@{body}

@{h2}What the check does

@{bullet}Placeholders @{tt}{{ name }}@{utt} in the string values of
@{tt}params@{utt}, also inside nested objects and lists, are replaced by
the variable's value as text -- the agent's @{tt}template_vars@{utt}
with the session's variables over them, so a value set with
@{" set_context " link set_context} counts. Only the plain form works, no
filters or expressions; a variable that is missing or null leaves the
placeholder as it is. A number becomes text: @{tt}"{{ id }}"@{utt} gives
@{tt}"42"@{utt}.
@{bullet}The tool is called directly: not through the agent's allowlist or
tool approval, and without the session, user or agent the loop would pass
it. It must work from its arguments alone.
@{bullet}The gate is open only when @{tt}gate_field@{utt} is exactly
@{tt}true@{utt} -- not "true", not 1. Otherwise the switch is blocked, and
the reason is the @{tt}error_field@{utt} value or "Gate 'can_proceed' is
not true for task 'execute'".
@{h2}A gate that cannot be checked is closed

@{table}
Case | The switch is blocked with the reason
the tool fails | Precondition check failed: <error>
the answer is not a JSON object | Gate tool 'project_status_check' for task 'execute' did not answer an object
the entry names no tool (or is not a mapping) | Gate for task 'execute' is misconfigured: no tool
@{body}

The last two are also logged as errors: they are mistakes in the
configuration, and the model cannot fix them -- the switch stays refused
until the entry is corrected. Only a call outside an agent (no agent in
the call) skips the check.
@endnode

@node set_context "Setting context variables"
@{h1}Setting context variables

@{tt}<instance>_set_context@{utt} sets any session variables -- an id the
later steps need, a mode, a name. They are the same variables a prompt
template and a @{" gate check " link gates} read, and the same the chat's
@{tt}/vars@{utt} command lists and changes (see
@{" Sessions " link scarabhive/sessions}).

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
vars | the variables as a JSON object in a string: @{tt}'{"project_id": 42, "mode": "review"}'@{utt}; an object is taken as well
@{body}

@{bullet}Values keep their JSON type: 42 stays a number, a list stays a
list.
@{bullet}Other top-level arguments next to @{tt}vars@{utt} are taken as
variables too; @{tt}vars@{utt} wins on the same name.
@{bullet}Names starting with @{tt}_@{utt}, and @{tt}request_id@{utt},
@{tt}requestId@{utt} and @{tt}vars@{utt}, are dropped.
@{bullet}Variables are only added or changed, never removed.
@{bullet}The task variable (@{tt}task_var_name@{utt}) is refused: it
changes only through set_task, with its allowed_tasks and gate checks.
The whole call is refused, the other variables in it are not set.

@{h2}Answers

@{table}
status | Fields
success | updated (what was set), previous (the old value per name, null when new), persisted (as for set_task)
error | see below
@{body}

@{table}
error | When
vars is not valid JSON: ... | the string does not parse
vars must be a JSON object (e.g. '{"book_id": 42}') | it parses to something else
No context variables provided. Pass them via the `vars` argument as a JSON object string, e.g. vars='{"book_id": 42}'. | nothing is left to set
'current_task' is the task variable: switch it with task_switch_set_task | the task variable is among them (named after the setting and the instance)
No session_tracker or agent_config available | called with no agent to set them on
@{body}

The status line shows @{i}Set: project_id=42, mode=review@{ui}, with
@{i}+N@{ui} for more than two.
@endnode

@node state "Where the state lives"
@{h1}Where the state lives

@{bullet}@{b}Per session@{ub}: every chat session has its own variables,
held in the memory of the agent that runs it and written into the
session record (@{tt}context_vars@{utt}). Two sessions of the same agent
do not see each other's task.
@{bullet}@{b}Restart@{ub}: the record survives. When a session is opened
again -- after a restart, in the web UI, on the command line, by a woken
run -- its variables are loaded from it. A new session starts from the
agent's @{tt}template_vars@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{b}Without a session@{ub} (a direct call that names none) the
agent's own @{tt}template_vars@{utt} are changed instead: shared by all of
that agent's sessions in the process, and gone at a restart. A warning is
logged.

@{h2}Sub-agents

@{bullet}A sub-agent runs in a session of its own. Its calls change its
own variables, never its parent's.
@{bullet}A sub-agent started by the sub-agent manager takes a copy of its
parent's variables when it is created -- the task variable included, when
both use the same name. When the parent continues it later, the parent's
current values are copied in again, except for the ones the sub-agent set
itself.
@{bullet}A set_context and a sub-agent start in the same turn: the
sub-agent gets the new value, whichever call comes first. The same holds
for set_task, except for a task with a gate check -- the check runs
first, and the sub-agent may start with the old task.

@{h2}How big it can grow

There is no limit on the number or size of variables. Each is kept in the
session record, and a variable the prompt template prints is sent with
every call. The memory entry goes when the session leaves the agent's
memory.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

The descriptions come from the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. With
@{tt}allowed_tasks@{utt} set, @{tt}task_name@{utt} offers exactly those
names; with any gate configured, the description of set_task adds that a
switch can be blocked. The answers and error texts are in
@{" Switching the task " link set_task} and
@{" Setting context variables " link set_context}.

@{h2}The prompt

The system prompt is rendered again before every LLM call, with the
session's variables over the agent's @{tt}template_vars@{utt}. A switch
shows from the next call on; the call that made it only gets the tool
answer. The plugin inserts no message into the history and rewrites none.
A variable named like a built-in template value (@{tt}tools@{utt},
@{tt}max_steps@{utt}, ...) replaces it in the prompt.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The system prompt is the head of every request, so the prompt cache
depends on it. A switch that changes what the template prints changes the
head: the next call reads the whole prompt again without the cache, once,
and it is cached anew from there. A variable the template does not print
changes nothing. An agent that switches back and forth pays that each
time; the tool answers themselves are appended like any other.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}The gate tool is called without the session: a tool that needs
to know the session or user cannot be a gate. That is deliberate -- the
gate is the operator's configuration, not the model's choice.
@{bullet}The server settings are read once; a change takes effect after a
restart.
@endnode
